{"id": "597b8d95-aeb9-4775-82be-511695cf0c8e", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T06:33:51.041737", "source": "user", "parent_id": "7c491234-67e2-4b91-aa3f-068025b250da", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach cleared with the following decisions and clarifications; the design must now be produced to spec. Approved setup assumptions: (1) Datasets - design is evaluated in two regimes, (a) synthetic sinusoidal+sine-of-sine+AR-noise series with known ground-truth periods in {8, 12, 16, 24, 48, 96, 168} (covers short/long and power-of-two/non-power-of-two) and (b) two real-data archetypes - regular-interval energy-style load (daily/weekly periodicity, L in {24, 168}) and regular-interval traffic-flow-like series (L in {24, 168}). Per-series train/val/test split = 70/10/20; rolling-origin evaluation window for backtesting is reported alongside the fixed split. Researcher shall name specific dataset IDs only if cited; otherwise designate them SYN-A, SYN-B, REAL-LOAD, REAL-TRAFFIC in the design. (2) Sampling - regular with documented native period L; if any source supplies irregular timestamps the experiment records resampling (mean-aggregate to L) and treats it as a preprocessing variant. (3) Horizon - H = L (primary) with secondary H-set = {L/8, L/4, L/2, L} when L/8 >= 4; both input look-back = 2L and L reported. (4) CycleNet backbone flexibility - the design treats CycleNet as needing a single canonical period per series at configuration time; a follow-up ablation explores a CycleNet variant with a small bag of cycle indices averaged, but only as design feasibility discussion, not as a run. (5) Ground-truth cycle definition - the dominant ACF peak; documented. (6) Channel aggregation - mean |ACF(c)| over channels with channels-z-scored before ACF; majority vote reported as a robustness sub-metric. (7) Preprocessing - two variants (raw, STL-detrended, season-length=candidate for STL); both reported. (8) Metrics - NRMSE and MASE vs seasonal-naive at the selected period; residual-lag-c Ljung-Box p-value as an auxiliary diagnostic. (9) Compute envelope - 24 CPU, no GPU; design therefore uses (a) ACF/FFT for cycle discovery, (b) Ljung-Box on a lightweight AR-X or Naive-baseline residuals at each candidate c for cheap residual-autocorrelation scoring, and (c) CycleNet re-training restricted to top-K=3 candidates plus random-3 baseline; this is the primary execution protocol. Required components of the final deliverable (no code execution, no fabricated numbers): A. Cover sheet (research question, single-sentence hypothesis, significance). B. Datasets and ground-truth table (SYN-A, SYN-B, REAL-LOAD, REAL-TRAFFIC with L, source class, train/val/test boundaries, rolling-origin count). C. Autocorrelation analysis pipeline - exact formulas for ACF (unbiased, FFT-computed), PACF (Yule-Walker up to order 2c), FFT magnitude spectrum (Hann window, zero-pad to 8x), STL with robust LOESS; pseudocode block; candidate construction c in {L, L/2, L/3, ... down to 4} U {2^k : 4 <= 2^k <= 4L} U {FFT top-3 prominent peaks rounded to nearest integer, clipped to [4, 4L]}. D. Mapping to CycleNet - how c candidate maps to the period-block hyperparameter; note the architectural assumption that block size = c (one block per cycle index) vs learnable cycle indices c_1..c_K; both listed as design variants with which one is PRIMARY. E. Scoring and selection rules - composite score per candidate: 0.4*norm_ACF_peak + 0.2*norm_FFT_prominence + 0.2*(1 - p_Ljung_Box_residual_at_c) + 0.2*norm_negative-log-p_PACF_at_c; channels aggregated by mean of |.|; top-K=3 advanced. The selection rule picks argmax; tie-break by smaller Ljung-Box p-value (i.e., more residual autocorrelation to remove). F. Controls - (i) random-cycle baseline (3 random c drawn without replacement from candidate set, best of 3 on val), (ii) seasonal-naive predictor at L (informational ceiling/floor), (iii) CycleNet at correctly-supplied L (oracle ceiling), (iv) CycleNet at L/2 and 2L (off-by-factor ablation). G. Analysis plan - primary endpoint = forecast NRMSE and MASE on test split for top-K vs random-3 vs oracle-L; secondary endpoint = rank correlation between composite score and validation NRMSE; tertiary endpoint = effect size (paired Wilcoxon across series) of top-1 vs random-best. Report also: discovered-c* vs true-L on SYN-A/B and discovered-c* vs documented-L on REAL-*. H. Feasibility - compute estimate: per series, ACF/PACF/FFT on 70% length is seconds; Ljung-Box over ~50 candidates is minutes; CycleNet re-train of 6 configs (top-3 + random-3) at L=24, H=24, channel=1 on 24 CPU is the budgeted workload; clearly below 24 CPU•hr envelope. I. Threats to validity - list (T1) co-existing cycles, (T2) non-stationarity, (T3) channel heterogeneity, (T4) bias of ACF toward L when L is short, (T5) CycleNet code constraints on period sizes, each with a mitigation referenced to a control. J. Deliverables list - final markdown document plus a one-page methods summary. Produce the full design now as a single markdown document, structured exactly per sections A-J above, with explicit formulas and pseudocode where requested; do NOT invent numerical results, do NOT simulate runs, and do NOT claim experiments were executed. End the document with exactly the line 'END_OF_DESIGN'.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
